in the average competitive player's head lives the myth of muscle memory as the holy grail of aim, like, you just need to rack up thousands of hours on one sens
so your hand makes perfect movements all by itself, without involving your brain. Memorize every centimeter of the pad, every tiny mouse movement, like a virtuoso violinist memorizing scales.

this idea is fucking dangerous cope, just like believing that "aim decides everything" (see [casualization of aim](/posts/games/casualization-of-aim)).

![](/pictures/aim/muscle_memory.png)

# # adaptation

the main misconception is thinking of muscle memory as a fixed recording in your neurons: In reality it's developing the ability to adapt effectively to different conditions and analyze them.

with aim, the brain and nervous system aren't learning to memorize mouse positions but to solve motor tasks: "the target is moving like this, need to apply this much force for this long".

# # sens schizophrenia

the cult's followers believe that finding their *perfect* sens (say, 52 cm/360) will let them reach the greatest heights and stuff, but the world isn't stable, what happens if:
- you switch your pad/skates to slightly slower/faster ones
- a speck of dust gets on your skates
- the weather procs higher humidity - the pad gets a little slower
- you've worn a slow spot into your pad, movement in the center is slower than around the edges

![](/pictures/aim/change_skates_gg.png)

any of these micro-changes completely invalidates the idea of memorized movements, with that *perfect* 52 cm/360 sens you actually need to apply
different amounts of force to make the same movements expecting the same result, and if you rely on pure muscle memory you'll miss
(even if you perfectly apply the force for a 10cm turn to the left - the mouse might only travel 9.5cm or a whole 10.5cm) -  the brain expected one level of resistance and got another.

# # the dumb basketball analogy that's been going around the community forever

a basketball player shooting at the hoop doesn't calculate millimeters, they judge distance, defenders' movement, their own acceleration and adapt the shot on the fly.

they can score from different positions, with different run-ups, with one or two hands, with wind or with a ball of a different weight and bounce - because they've developed overall coordination.

same way a cracked aimer doesn't remember how many centimeters to move the mouse for a headshot and stuff - they see the target, their brain instantly judges the distance from the crosshair to the target and how fast the target is moving,
and their arm, wrist and fingers cooperate to solve that task, the makeup of this "cooperation" changes depending on sens.

![](/pictures/aim/plecho.png)

- on low sens (50 cm/360)** the forearm and upper arm do most of the work for big turns, while the wrist and fingers make micro-corrections.
- on high sens (20 cm/360)** the wrist and fingers get actively involved, upper arm and forearm movement decreases.

the perfect aimer is someone who's developed the neuromotor control that lets them use any sens effectively to solve the task.

depending on the task - sens can vary - a fast sens will be more comfortable for arena shooters, a slow one for tactical shooters.

![](/pictures/aim/sens_chart.png)

# # the visuomotor loop

hours of jerking off in aim trainers train the speed and efficiency of the feedback loop:
1. the eye sees the target and its movement.
2. the brain processes the visual information and calculates the required motor command.
3. the hand executes the command.
4. repeat the cycle, analyze the previous results

the point of training is to shorten the time it takes to go through this loop (reduce analysis time) and improve calculation accuracy in step 2.

this isn't about muscle memory - it's a calibration skill, not a reproduction skill.

# # conclusion

obsessing over muscle memory and finding "the one" sens is a dead end that creates a fragile system that collapses at the slightest change in conditions.

the tru aim skill is hand-eye coordination.

with regular aim training - changing sens stops being noticeable - you can freely jump between 24-34-38-44-55-66-77-88 *(etc. etc.)* cm/360 without really feeling much difference - adapting to the new conditions will take mere seconds.
